Here is the updated logic for both sides to ensure this "silent" handshake works perfectly.

1. The React Artifact Fix (The "One-Word" Change)
As you noted, changing the header to text/plain tells the browser this is a "simple request," allowing it to sail through without needing a preflight approval.

JavaScript
const saveJobsToSheet = async (jobs) => {
  const WEB_APP_URL = 'YOUR_DEPLOYED_URL_HERE';

  try {
    // We use text/plain to avoid triggering a CORS preflight (OPTIONS) request
    // which is blocked by the Claude sandbox.
    await fetch(WEB_APP_URL, {
      method: 'POST',
      mode: 'no-cors', 
      headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'text/plain', // <--- THE FIX
      },
      body: JSON.stringify(jobs),
    });
    
    // Note: With no-cors, the response is 'opaque', so we assume success 
    // if no exception is thrown by the fetch itself.
    return { success: true };
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Network error or sandbox block:", error);
    throw error;
  }
};
2. The Google Apps Script (Verified)
Since e.postData.contents captures the raw string regardless of the header, your original logic is robust. I’ve cleaned it up slightly to ensure it handles that stringified JSON perfectly.

JavaScript
function doPost(e) {
  try {
    // e.postData.contents gets the raw string from the 'text/plain' fetch
    const payload = JSON.parse(e.postData.contents);
    const ss = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet();
    const sheet = ss.getSheets()[0];
    
    const jobs = Array.isArray(payload) ? payload : [payload];
    
    jobs.forEach(job => {
      sheet.appendRow([
        job.title || "",
        job.company || "",
        job.location || "",
        job.url || "",
        job.date || "",
        job.posted || "",
        job.source || "",
        job.sweepDate || new Date().toLocaleDateString(),
        job.status || "New",
        job.shortMessage || "",
        job.coverLetter || ""
      ]);
    });

    return ContentService.createTextOutput(JSON.stringify({ "status": "success" }))
      .setMimeType(ContentService.MimeType.JSON);

  } catch (error) {
    return ContentService.createTextOutput(JSON.stringify({ "status": "error", "message": error.toString() }))
      .setMimeType(ContentService.MimeType.JSON);
  }
}
Why this solves it:
No Preflight: By using text/plain, the browser doesn't send an OPTIONS request. It just fires the POST immediately.

Opaque Success: In no-cors mode, you won't be able to read the response body (it will be "opaque"), but the data will reach Google and the script will execute.

Bypassing the Sandbox: Since the Claude environment is essentially a highly restrictive iframe, sticking to "Simple Requests" (GET, HEAD, or POST with safelisted headers) is the only reliable way to send data out without a backend.
